+// The USBD has a single EasyDMA channel: only one transfer between an
+// endpoint buffer and RAM may run at a time, and tasks triggered while one
+// runs may be ignored (anomaly 199). Transfers are therefore started with
+// startDMA, which first waits for the running one to end.
+//
+// The wait polls the end event instead of sleeping: it may run in the USBD
+// interrupt handler, where the interrupt that would wake a wfi cannot be
+// taken. A transfer of one packet ends within microseconds; the timeout
+// only guards against a transfer cut short by a bus reset.
+
+// startDMA triggers task, which starts a transfer that ends with event end,
+// once the running transfer has ended. Interrupts must be disabled or the
+// caller must be the USBD interrupt handler.
+func startDMA(task, end *volatile.Register32) {
+	waitDMA()
+	end.Set(0)
+	dmaEnd = end
+	// anomaly 199: keep the USBD able to receive tasks during DMA
+	(*volatile.Register32)(unsafe.Pointer(uintptr(0x40027C1C))).Set(0x00000082)
+	task.Set(1)
+}
+
+// waitDMA waits for the running transfer, if any, to end. It leaves the end
+// event set for its handler.
+func waitDMA() {
+	if dmaEnd == nil {
+		return
 	}
